      "updated": 1774107485220,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) CSRF (Cross site Request Forgery)\n- User is already authenticated to a site.\n-CSRF attack tricks a browser into making unwanted request to a site where user is already authenticated.\n- For example when SSO is authenticated and you click on some suspicious link and from here attacker will make unwanted request \nusing cookie and sessionId.\n- Applicable where state and session is managed.\n\nProtection from this :\n- By using CSRF token, this ensures that request originates from the legitimate source. As authenticate forms or website only \nappend the CSRF token in the request. Which server can used to validate with the token created at the time of HTTP session \ncreation.\n\n2) XSS (Cross-site Scripting)\n- It allows attacker to put malicious script into web page viewed by other users.\n- Like comments section page.\n- Commonly used for stealing the session or deform the website.\n\nHow to get protected from XSS attack :\n- By proper escaping user input(converting special character like < to &lt)\n- By properly validating data before rendering.\n\n3) CORS (Cross-origin Resource Sharing)\n- Its not an attack but more of a security feature that restrict web pages from making request to different origin, unless\nallowed by the server.\nDifferent origin = protocol + domain + port\nSo, whenever there is a call between different origin, server has to allow :\n- By setting \"Access-Control-Allow-Origin\" and other header to allow the cross-origin request.\n\n4) SQL Injection\n- In this attacker manipulates SQL query by inserting malicious input into the user field.\n\nProtection from SQL Injection attack :\n- By parameterized Query\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 843,
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) CSRF (Cross site Request Forgery)\n- User is already authenticated to a site.\n-CSRF attack tricks a browser into making unwanted request to a site where user is already authenticated.\n- For example when SSO is authenticated and you click on some suspicious link and from here attacker will make unwanted request \nusing cookie and sessionId.\n- Applicable where state and session is managed.\n\nProtection from this :\n- By using CSRF token, this ensures that request originates from the legitimate source. As authenticate forms or website only \nappend the CSRF token in the request. Which server can used to validate with the token created at the time of HTTP session \ncreation.\n\n2) XSS (Cross-site Scripting)\n- It allows attacker to put malicious script into web page viewed by other users.\n- Like comments section page.\n- Commonly used for stealing the session or deform the website.\n\nHow to get protected from XSS attack :\n- By proper escaping user input(converting special character like < to &lt)\n- By properly validating data before rendering.\n\n3) CORS (Cross-origin Resource Sharing)\n- Its not an attack but more of a security feature that restrict web pages from making request to different origin, unless\nallowed by the server.\nDifferent origin = protocol + domain + port\nSo, whenever there is a call between different origin, server has to allow :\n- By setting \"Access-Control-Allow-Origin\" and other header to allow the cross-origin request.\n\n4) SQL Injection\n- In this attacker manipulates SQL query by inserting malicious input into the user field.\n\nProtection from SQL Injection attack :\n- By parameterized Query\n",
